            <ArticleTitle>Effects of the Neurac(®) technique in patients with acute-phase subacromial impingement syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1407-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1589/jpts.27.1407</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>[Purpose] This study investigated the effects of the Neurac technique on shoulder pain, function, and range of motion in patients with acute-phase subacromial impingement syndrome. [Subjects] Thirteen patients (seven females and six males) with acute-phase subacromial impingement syndrome participated in this study. [Methods] Shoulder pain, function, and range of motion were assessed before and after the application of the Neurac technique. [Results] Pain and function scores were significantly lower after than before the Neurac intervention. Shoulder range of motion was significantly greater after Neurac intervention than before it. [Conclusion] The Neurac technique is a useful intervention for patients with acute-phase subacromial impingement syndrome.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
